Z||000014||Hippocrat||10-26-2001||12:11 AM||||If you wanted to make it a huge PITA you could actually still fasten the panels with some small screws before filling in the grooves if you were concerned about the rattles. Just hope that if you have to take them off at any point, it is to replace them.<P>I'm looking for something to fill the groove in as well haven't decided on anything yet though.||||reg||1
Z||000015||8Ball||10-26-2001||12:46 AM||||Ya know I had a thought. Not sure if it would work but.. If you have ever seen the plastic slide in support posts like some motherboards used, You will see what I am saying. I wanna see if I can take a dremell and cut slotted holes where the panel would bolt on in the middle. And maybe use the posts to slide down in the middle to hold it there. Then you would just have to unscrew the top and bottom of the panel, slide it up and remove it.<P>What to ya think?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Hippocrat||10-26-2001||01:04 AM||||Thats a decent idea. Thats pretty much how the door is done on my 86 (a little different and larger fitting) I have to pull the quarter off one this weekend. Ill take a look to see if it would work. The doors arent an issue there arent any fasteners other than at the top and one in the middle and the bottom screws.<P>Gonna buy some different types of goop to test out filling in the groove on the old parts car.<BR>||||reg||1
